+---
+
+## Entry 10
+
+### Status
+Ran a second external review pass against this exact commit (the one Entry 9 above produced). Good news first: nearly everything Entry 9 fixed actually held up — Fiji's DEM artifact, the Lakshadweep placeholder zero, the seasonal MK switch, the NDBI wording, the mangrove count typo, all still correct on a fresh check. But this pass caught two real wording/labeling problems I'd genuinely missed, nd one data-provenance gap tht was disclosed but not disclosed prominently enough. None of these touch a single number in any CSV — they're all about whether the words around the numbers accurately describe wht the numbers are.
+
+### The Compound Score's Coral Input Was Called a "Trend" — It Isn't One
+
+This one's subtle but real. `compound_vulnerability_score.py` has always used `coral_dhw_increase` — the early-vs-recent period DHW comparison from Section 3.5 — as the coral input to the Compound Vulnerability Score. Thts a different thing from the seasonal Mann-Kendall trend test Entry 9 fixed, which is a full-24-year-series robustness check, not an input to the score itself. The code comment even said "early vs recent period" the whole time, so the code was never confused abt this.
+
+The writing was, though. The paper's Section 3.6, the Figure 5 caption, the README headline findings, the Executive Summary methodology line, nd four separate dashboard pages all described the compound score as combining physical exposure with a coral thermal-stress "trend" — the same word Section 4.3 uses for the actual MK test. A reader going only off the prose would reasonably think Seychelles' high compound score was driven by its statistically-significant MK trend result, when it's actually driven by its period-comparison DHW change (+0.68°C-weeks, the largest in the sample either way, so the substantive story doesn't change — jst the label was wrong).
+
+Fixed by renaming this consistently to "coral thermal-stress change" (or spelling out "early-vs-recent period DHW increase") everywhere it refers to the compound score's actual input, nd adding an explicit one-line distinction in Section 3.6 nd the Figure 5 caption pointing at Section 4.3 as the separate trend-test result. Reserved "trend" language for places tht are actually describing the MK test. Touched: `DJ_Research_Paper.md` (§3.6, abstract, Figure 5 caption), `README.md`, `DJ_Executive_Summary.md`, nd dashboard pages 1, 4, 5, nd 7.
+
+### The Governance Ratio's Name Implied a Percentage It Isn't
+
+The 10km-coastal-buffer WDPA fix from Entry 6 was the right fix for the old 1005.69 EEZ bug — but I'd never actually checked whether the resulting number could still mislead someone on its own terms. It can: Seychelles' ratio is 11.32, because the buffer's WDPA-in-buffer numerator can include marine protected area near the coast, not jst land. Calling tht a "Coastal WDPA Protection Ratio" or "coastal protected-area coverage" reads like a percentage of island area under protection, which it never was nd can't be once it's over 1.0.
+
+Renamed it to "WDPA Coastal-Buffer Ratio" everywhere (paper §3.7 nd §4.5, README, both the dashboard governance page nd its plotly axis label, nd the matplotlib Figure 6 ylabel/title/caption), nd added an explicit sentence at first use in §3.7 nd on the dashboard page spelling out wht the numerator nd denominator actually are nd why Seychelles' number is above 1.0. Nothing abt the actual ratio values (11.32 / 3.90 / 2.32 / 0.42 / 0.00) or the r=0.862, p=0.138, n=4 correlation changed — jst wht the axis nd the prose call it.
+
+### Figure 3's Population-Weighted Bars Had a Comment Tht Contradicted My Own Script
+
+`population_weighted_exposure.py`'s docstring has said since Entry 9 tht its v5 boundary-polygon fix exists in code but hasn't been rerun against the real rasters, so the numbers in the paper are still the v4 bbox-only ones. Tht caveat was correctly in the paper's table footnote nd Limitations section, nd in the Executive Summary's robustness checklist. But `research_paper_figures.py`'s Figure 3 function had a comment claiming these were "from population_weighted_exposure.py's validated final run" — which flatly contradicts wht tht script's own docstring says. I have no idea when tht comment nd the docstring drifted apart, but they clearly did, nd whoever (me) wrote the figure comment wasn't looking at the script it was describing.
+
+README's "Findings hold up under robustness checks" line had the same gap in a smaller way — it presented the population-weighted recomputation as a clean confirmation, no caveat attached, even though the Executive Summary's checklist right next to it flagged the same thing correctly with a "!" instead of a "✓".
+
+Fixed three ways: (1) moved the hardcoded `population_pct` dict in `research_paper_figures.py` into a new `data/population_weighted_exposure_summary.csv`, tagged with a `method` column (`bbox_v4_pending_polygon_mask_rerun`) so it's obvious at a glance these aren't final, nd Figure 3 now reads from tht CSV instead of a hardcoded dict; (2) rewrote the misleading comment, nd added a note directly to Figure 3's caption text (so it shows up on the actual chart image, not jst in surrounding prose) tht these bars are bbox-based pending a rerun; (3) reworded README's robustness line to state the caveat inline instead of implying a clean confirmation. Updated `population_weighted_exposure.py`'s docstring to point at the new CSV nd say wht to update once it's actually rerun with the real rasters.
+
+### Where This Leaves Things
+
+Every number in every CSV, every p-value, every compound score, every WDPA ratio — all unchanged from Entry 9. This round was entirely about making sure the words describing those numbers say wht the numbers actually are, not wht they sound like they might be. Two genuine labeling mismatches (coral "trend" vs "change", WDPA "protection ratio" vs "coastal-buffer ratio") nd one disclosure tht existed but wasn't visible enough in the one place (README) most likely to be read on its own.
