+A ten-kilometer coastal buffer around each island, not total captured protected-area extent, is what I used to quantify coverage — total extent would pull in large offshore marine zones only loosely relevant to settlement-level risk for some islands. (An earlier version of this calculation divided each island's *entire* WDPA extent — marine and terrestrial together — by land area alone, which produced an impossible Seychelles ratio of over 1000x; the 10km coastal-buffer approach is the corrected version, and the earlier scripts are kept in the repository only as a record of that bug, not as a live analysis step. See Section 6.) This buffer-based ratio is still not a percentage of island land under formal protection: the numerator is whatever WDPA area (marine or terrestrial) falls inside that 10km coastal buffer, so a small, mostly-water island with large nearby marine protected areas can produce a ratio above 1.0 — Seychelles' 11.32 being the clearest case. I read it as a coastal-buffer protection-intensity measure, not a land-coverage share. I tested that buffer-based figure against the Compound Vulnerability Score using a Pearson correlation. Lakshadweep had no available WDPA protected-area layer to clip against this buffer; rather than substitute a zero for the missing value, I excluded Lakshadweep from the correlation itself (n=4) and report its vulnerability score separately, since a fabricated zero would bias an already-small five-point correlation (see Section 6).
 
